      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The seesaw analogy was just a way to illustrate the balance between the pitot tube and static port. Let's get to the technical aspect.\n\nIf the pitot tube gets blocked, the pressure difference between the front and rear of the tube is disrupted. This causes the airspeed indicator to read a higher airspeed than the actual speed of the aircraft. The blockage essentially \"short-circuits\" the pressure differential, allowing the airspeed indicator to read a higher speed than it would normally.\n\nThink of it like a goblin trying to sneak into a narrow tunnel - if the tunnel is blocked, the goblin can't get through, but the airspeed indicator thinks the aircraft is moving faster than it actually is. This can lead to a range of problems, including loss of control, reduced fuel efficiency, and even engine failure.\n\nIn the case of a blockage, the airspeed indicator will typically read a higher speed than the actual airspeed, and may even show a \"stall\" or \"spike\" in the airspeed reading. This is because the airspeed indicator is trying to compensate for the lack of pressure data from the pitot tube, but it's essentially making an incorrect assumption about the aircraft's speed.",
